  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "If ${log}_{5}⁡(a-10)=3$, then $a=$",
  "worked_solution": "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(a-10) & =3 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)} & ={5}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)}=a-10.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-10 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ a-10 & =125 \\\\ a & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
